│   └── rignite_tap_engine.py
│       ├── RigniteClient: Main game loop and HTTP API communicator.
│       ├── HumanTapTelemetry: Mathematical simulation of front-end touch `go()` tapfp algorithm.
│       ├── simulate_rewarded_ad(): 2-step ad intent/complete handshake.
│       └── emit_telemetry(): Flushes structured `[AUTOPLAYER_METRIC]` JSON to stdout.
│
├── CATIZEN/
│   ├── play_catizen.js: WebSocket game agent handling board state and cat merges.
│   ├── pb_bundle.js: Protocol Buffers binary codec for Catizen network frames.
│   └── CATIZEN_PROTOCOL_GUIDE.md: Packet layout and opcode dictionary.
│
└── logs/
    ├── catizen_history.log: Catizen rolling console logs.
    ├── rignite_history.log: Rignite rolling console logs.
    └── events_audit.jsonl: Structured audit event log in JSON Lines format.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. INTER-PROCESS COMMUNICATION (IPC) SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The supervisor (`auto_player.py`) executes sub-engines as child processes using
`subprocess.Popen(..., stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)`.

Child processes communicate metrics to the supervisor by writing a single line to stdout:
`[AUTOPLAYER_METRIC] {"game": "<game_name>", "type": "<event_type>", ...}`

Protocol Rules for AI Modifying or Adding Engines:
1. Always prefix telemetry JSON with `[AUTOPLAYER_METRIC] `.
2. Flush stdout immediately (`sys.stdout.flush()` in Python or `process.stdout.write()` in Node).
3. Payload schema standard:
   {
       "game": "rignite" | "catizen",
       "type": "tap_sync" | "merge" | "recharge_start" | "ad_watching" | "auth_success",
       "user": "<telegram_username>",
       "coins" / "gold": <int>,
       "energy": <int>,
       "max_energy": <int>,
       "action_status": "<human_readable_action>",
       "next_action": "<countdown_or_next_step>",
       "rate_cps": <float>,
       "rate_cpm": <int>,
       "coins_delta": <int>,
       "session_taps": <int>,
       "timestamp": <epoch_ms>
   }

When `auto_player.py` reads a line containing `[AUTOPLAYER_METRIC]`:
- It extracts the JSON payload and routes it to `tracker.record_metric(data)`.
- It suppresses the raw metric string from the human-facing log buffer.
- Standard stdout strings (without the marker) are written to `tracker.add_log(line)`
  and persisted to the game's log file in `logs/`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. RIGNITE TAP ENGINE REVERSE-ENGINEERED SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Target Service: `https://api.rignite.app`
Auth Source: Telegram Mini App `initData` via `https://tg-game-sessionmaker.onrender.com`

Auth Protocol:
- Headers:
  - `x-init-data`: Raw URL-encoded Telegram WebApp initData string
  - `content-type`: `application/json`
  - `x-rignite-ad-policy`: `adexium_house:2026-09-06.1`
- Request: `POST /auth` with `{"lang": "en", "dev": "<uuid4>", "fp": "<YYYY-MM-DD>"}`
- Response contains: `sessionId` (used in subsequent headers as `x-session`), `user` profile.

Tapping Protocol (`POST /tap`):
- Payload: `{"count": <accepted_taps>}` (usually 14-24 taps per batch).
- Anti-Cheat Telemetry:
  The engine simulates `HumanTapTelemetry`:
  - `ivort`: Average tap interval in ms (120ms - 240ms).
  - `ivcv`: Coefficient of variation of tap intervals (0.12 - 0.35).
  - `xystd`: Spatial variance across button coordinates.
  - `imza`: 32-bit hash computed via rolling prime multiplier (x31 >>> 0).
  - Alternation: Organic swapping between Left (`L`) and Right (`R`) poles (~88%).
  - At 500 taps, sends authentic telemetry payload to `POST /client-log`.

Energy & Refill Lifecycle:
- Energy capacity defaults to 1000 - 3500 points.
- When energy drops <= 15:
  1. Instant Daily Boost: Checks `fullEnergyLeft > 0`. If available, calls `POST /boost/full-energy`.
  2. Rewarded Ads: If daily boost is depleted, uses `POST /ad/intent` (type: `full_energy`),
     simulates viewing for 16-25s, then dispatches `POST /ad/complete` with valid timing metadata.
  3. Natural Recharge: If ads are in cooldown or depleted, sleeps 60-150s while battery recharges,
     emitting live countdown metrics every 4s.

Session Recovery:
- Listens for HTTP 401 Unauthorized -> triggers `fetch_fresh_init_data()` and re-authenticates.
- Listens for HTTP 429 Too Many Requests -> parses `x-ratelimit-reset` and sleeps.
- Proactively refreshes the session token every 40 minutes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. CATIZEN PROTOCOL & MERGE SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Protocol Stack: WebSockets over TLS (`wss://`) with binary Protocol Buffers encoding.

Board Mechanics:
- 12 slots arranged in a 3x4 grid.
- Each cat on board has a level (e.g., Lvl 1 to Lvl 50+).
- Two cats of equal level can be merged to yield one cat of `level + 1`.
- The engine identifies matching pairs on the board, generates the binary drag-and-merge
  packet, and emits it over the WebSocket.
- Automatically buys low-level cats with accumulated gold whenever slots are vacant.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WEB DASHBOARD API & CONTROL CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The dashboard server runs on `http://0.0.0.0:<WEB_PORT>` (default: 8080).

Endpoints:
1. `GET /`: Returns single-file HTML/CSS/JS dashboard (`WEB_DASHBOARD_HTML`).
2. `GET /api/status`:
   Returns JSON payload:
   {
       "uptime": "2h 15m 30s",
       "countdown": "35m 12s",
       "tg_status": "Online",
       "catizen": {
           "status": "ONLINE & ACTIVE" | "PAUSED BY USER" | "RECONNECTING",
           "enabled": true | false,
           "web_app_url": "<signed_game_url>",
           "gold": <int>,
           "gold_delta": <int>,
           "gold_rate": <int>,
           "board": [0, 1, 1, 2, ...], // 12 elements
           "logs": [{"time": "21:30:00", "text": "..."}]
       },
       "rignite": {
           "status": "ONLINE & ACTIVE" | "PAUSED BY USER",
           "enabled": true | false,
           "coins": <int>,
           "coins_delta": <int>,
           "coins_rate": <float>,
           "coins_cpm": <int>,
           "energy": <int>,
           "max_energy": <int>,
           "battery_energy": <int>,
           "battery_cap": <int>,
           "session_taps": <int>,
           "tap_cadence": <float>,
           "boosts": <int>,
           "ad_energy_left": <int>,
           "action_status": "<status_text>",
           "next_action": "<countdown_or_next_step>",
           "logs": [{"time": "21:30:00", "text": "..."}]
       }
   }

3. `POST /api/control?game=<catizen|rignite>&action=<stop|resume>`:
   - `action=stop`: Sets `tracker.is_enabled = False`, terminates child process handle.
   - `action=resume`: Sets `tracker.is_enabled = True`, calls `reset_metrics_baseline()`.
     CRITICAL: `reset_metrics_baseline()` resets `start_state` and `start_coins` to prevent
     spent coins or gold during pauses from skewing deltas and velocity calculations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. BEST PRACTICES FOR EXTENDING OR DEBUGGING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Modifying auto_player.py:
   - Keep `WEB_DASHBOARD_HTML` self-contained (inline CSS & JS) so it runs cleanly
     in headless containers without separate asset hosting.
   - Never block the supervisor thread. Subprocess I/O must remain in dedicated threads.
   - Always run `python -m py_compile auto_player.py` before committing.

2. Modifying Rignite Engine:
   - Never send static or flat intervals; always pass tap batches through `HumanTapTelemetry`.
   - Ensure all new action states emit telemetry with `action_status` and `next_action`
     so the Web Dashboard refill banner reflects real-time status.

3. Git & Deployment:
   - Repository: `https://github.com/appujohn007/Auto-TG_Gameplay.git`
   - Default branch: `main`
   - Use clean conventional commit messages (e.g. `feat: ...`, `fix: ...`, `docs: ...`).
